From 0921b9fd5c776aec7748475c6c10807e0d51bc6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vasily Sviridov <sviridovcomp@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 May 2026 23:20:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: re-apply BOOST_NOINLINE to manage_exception_state
 (reverted in #324 merge)

Backport of upstream boostorg/context commit
0921b9fd5c776aec7748475c6c10807e0d51bc6d, which is missing from boost
1.89 (the BOOST_NOINLINE was accidentally reverted by the merge of
upstream PR #324, and 1.89 was released in that state).

Without it, __cxa_get_globals() -- declared __attribute__((const)) --
gets CSE'd by the compiler across the context switch between the
constructor and the destructor of manage_exception_state. When a fiber
suspends on one thread and is resumed on another, the destructor then
restores the exception-state snapshot through the *original* thread's
globals pointer, silently corrupting that thread's live libstdc++
exception state (caughtExceptions/uncaughtExceptions) from another
thread. This caused reproducible "std::terminate() called without
exception" aborts and segfaults in the parallel evaluator whenever
multiple fibers unwound exceptions concurrently. BOOST_NOINLINE keeps
the two calls in separate non-inlined functions, so each fetches the
globals of the thread it actually runs on.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/boost/context/fiber_fcontext.hpp
+++ b/boost/context/fiber_fcontext.hpp
@@ -89,7 +89,7 @@
 
 class manage_exception_state {
 public:
-    manage_exception_state() {
+    BOOST_NOINLINE manage_exception_state() {
         exception_state_ = *__cxa_get_globals();
     }
     // Hack to account for the forced_unwind exception thrown in fiber_unwind
@@ -97,7 +97,7 @@
     void from_forced_unwind() noexcept {
         exception_state_.uncaughtExceptions += 1;
     }
-    ~manage_exception_state() {
+    BOOST_NOINLINE ~manage_exception_state() {
         *__cxa_get_globals() = exception_state_;
     }
 private:
